{"graph_chars": "When the wage is not at equilibrium, incentives push it there. A surplus of applicants, and employers offer less. A shortage, and they offer more, and others match to keep their staff. The demand for labor is derived demand: it comes from demand for the product. More cars, more autoworkers. More restaurant meals, more chefs. More prescriptions, more pharmacists. More legal services, more attorneys. Table four point two, the other labor-demand shifters. Education and training: a more productive workforce, demand right. Technology: a substitute, like word processing for typists, demand left. A complement, like software for I T staff, demand right. Number of companies: more firms, demand right. Government rules: if only nurses may do a procedure, demand for nurses right, demand for less-trained aides left. Price of other inputs: cheaper phones at a call center, production more profitable, demand for labor right.", "graph_times": [[0.0, 0.0708], [0.0708, 0.1416], [0.1416, 0.2123], [0.2123, 0.2831], [0.2831, 0.3539], [0.3539, 0.4247], [0.4247, 0.4955], [0.4955, 0.5662], [0.5662, 0.637], [0.637, 0.7078], [0.7078, 0.7786], [0.7786, 0.8494], [0.8494, 0.9202], [0.9202, 0.9909], [0.9909, 1.0617], [1.0617, 1.1325], [1.1325, 1.2033], [1.2033, 1.2741], [1.2741, 1.3448], [1.3448, 1.4156], [1.4156, 1.4864], [1.4864, 1.5572], [1.5572, 1.628], [1.628, 1.6987], [1.6987, 1.7695], [1.7695, 1.8403], [1.8403, 1.9111], [1.9111, 1.9819], [1.9819, 2.0526], [2.0526, 2.1234], [2.1234, 2.1942], [2.1942, 2.265], [2.265, 2.3358], [2.3358, 2.4066], [2.4066, 2.4773], [2.4773, 2.5481], [2.5481, 2.6189], [2.6189, 2.6897], [2.6897, 2.7605], [2.7605, 2.8312], [2.8312, 2.902], [2.902, 2.9728], [2.9728, 3.0436], [3.0436, 3.1144], [3.1144, 3.1851], [3.1851, 3.2559], [3.2559, 3.3267], [3.3267, 3.3975], [3.3975, 3.4683], [3.4683, 3.539], [3.539, 3.6098], [3.6098, 3.6806], [3.6806, 3.7514], [3.7514, 3.8222], [3.8222, 3.893], [3.893, 3.9637], [3.9637, 4.0345], [4.0345, 4.1053], [4.1053, 4.17